<section xml:id="sec-translation-vector" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Translation Vector</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.2 Vectors
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Draw an image of an object under a translation vector on a Cartesian plane
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Translation vector: drawing the image of an object under a translation.
         Suggested activity: Learners translate a shape on squared paper using a given translation vector.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Draggable triangle with a translation vector set by sliders; the image moves as the vector changes. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Essential 2.2 (f); (g) in the closing paragraphs
         Activity: as planning notes (translate a triangle on squared paper)
         JSXGraph: fig-translation-vector-triangle (assets/jsxgraph/translation-vector-triangle.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/11-vectors/07-translation-vector/ -->

    <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
        <ol>
            <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a translation moves every point by the same vector; the translation vector is image minus object; the image is the same size and shape and faces the same way.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the image of a point and of a shape; find the translation vector from an object and its image; find an object from its image.</p></li>
            <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> road arrows painted along a lane; the stripes of a zebra crossing.</p></li>
        </ol>
    </p>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
            <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners translate a triangle and see that every vertex moves by the same column vector.</p></li>
            <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give out squared paper and a cut-out triangle to slide. After part (b), ask: <q>Did any vertex move differently from the others?</q></p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-sliding-a-triangle">
        <title>Sliding a Triangle</title>
        <statement>
            <p>Draw the triangle with vertices <m>A(1, 1)</m>, <m>B(4, 1)</m> and <m>C(1, 3)</m>. Slide it <m>5</m> squares right and <m>2</m> squares up: the move <m>\mathbf{T} = \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>Draw the new triangle and label its vertices <m>A'</m>, <m>B'</m> and <m>C'</m>.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>A'(6, 3)</m>, <m>B'(9, 3)</m>, <m>C'(6, 5)</m>; see <xref ref="fig-translation-triangle"/>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Write <m>\overrightarrow{AA'}</m>, <m>\overrightarrow{BB'}</m> and <m>\overrightarrow{CC'}</m> as column vectors.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>All three are <m>\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Compare the side lengths of the two triangles, and the way they face.</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>The sides are the same lengths, and the new triangle faces the same way: it has not turned or flipped.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Which move takes the new triangle back to the first one?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>Where on the roads near your school have you seen one painted shape repeated by sliding it?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Arrows painted along a lane; the stripes of a zebra crossing.</p></solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <figure xml:id="fig-translation-triangle">
        <caption>Triangle <m>ABC</m> and its image <m>A'B'C'</m> under the translation by <m>\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</caption>
        <image width="55%">
            <shortdescription>Triangle A(1, 1), B(4, 1), C(1, 3) and its image A'(6, 3), B'(9, 3), C'(6, 5), with equal arrows from each vertex to its image.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.6]
\draw[very thin, gray!40] (0,0) grid (10,6);
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (10.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,6.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {2,4,6,8,10} \node[below] at (\x,0) {\scriptsize \x};
\foreach \y in {2,4,6} \node[left] at (0,\y) {\scriptsize \y};
\draw[very thick] (1,1) -- (4,1) -- (1,3) -- cycle;
\draw[very thick, dashed] (6,3) -- (9,3) -- (6,5) -- cycle;
\foreach \p/\q in {{(1,1)}/{(6,3)}, {(4,1)}/{(9,3)}, {(1,3)}/{(6,5)}} \draw[thin, -{Stealth}] \p -- \q;
\node[below left] at (1,1) {$A$}; \node[below right] at (4,1) {$B$}; \node[above left] at (1,3) {$C$};
\node[below left] at (6,3) {$A'$}; \node[below right] at (9,3) {$B'$}; \node[above left] at (6,5) {$C'$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>To formalise translations and the translation vector.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (b) shows every vertex moves by the same vector <m>\mathbf{T}</m>.</p></li>
                <li><p>Add <m>\mathbf{T}</m> to every vertex; <m>\mathbf{T}</m> is image minus object; <m>-\mathbf{T}</m> undoes the translation (part (d)).</p></li>
                <li><p>A translated shape is the same size and shape and is not turned (part (c)).</p></li>
            </ol>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <insight xml:id="insight-translations">
        <title>Translations</title>
        <p>
            A <term>translation</term> slides a shape without turning it: every point of the shape, the <term>object</term>, moves by the same vector to give the <term>image</term>. That vector is the <term>translation vector</term> <m>\mathbf{T}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Under the translation by the vector <m>\mathbf{T} = \begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix}</m>, the point <m>P(x, y)</m> moves to <m>P'(x + a, y + b)</m>. Image points are labelled with a dash: <m>P'</m> is read <q>P dash</q>. The translation vector is image minus object, <m>\mathbf{T} = \overrightarrow{PP'}</m>, and the translation by <m>-\mathbf{T}</m> takes the image back to the object.
        </p>
        <p>
            The image has the same size and shape as the object and faces the same way.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-translation-vector-triangle">
        <caption>Drag the vertices of the triangle and move the <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> sliders. Every vertex moves by the same vector <m>\mathbf{T}</m>.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-translation" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="6:7" source="jsxgraph/translation-vector-triangle.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-translation" surface="jsxboard" aspect="6:7"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Move the <alert>a</alert> slider. Which way does the image move when <alert>a</alert> is negative?</p></li>
                        <li><p>Set <alert>a</alert> and <alert>b</alert> so that the image of <m>A</m> is at the origin.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Make the image land on the triangle again. What is <m>\mathbf{T}</m> then?</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example xml:id="example-image-of-a-shape">
        <title>Image of a Shape</title>
        <statement>
            <p>The quadrilateral <m>PQRS</m> has vertices <m>P(-3, 1)</m>, <m>Q(0, 1)</m>, <m>R(0, 3)</m> and <m>S(-2, 4)</m>. Find its image under the translation by the vector <m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Add <m>4</m> to each <m>x</m>-coordinate and <m>-3</m> to each <m>y</m>-coordinate:</p>
            <p><m>P'(1, -2)</m>, <m>Q'(4, -2)</m>, <m>R'(4, 0)</m>, <m>S'(2, 1)</m>.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="example-finding-the-translation-vector">
        <title>Finding the Translation Vector</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A translation maps <m>K(2, -1)</m> to <m>K'(-3, 2)</m>. Find the translation vector, check it with <m>L(4, 0)</m> and <m>L'(-1, 3)</m>, and write the translation that takes the image back.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Image minus object: <m>\mathbf{T} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 - 2 \\ 2 - (-1) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
            <p>Check: <m>L(4, 0)</m> moves to <m>(4 - 5, 0 + 3) = (-1, 3)</m>, which is <m>L'</m>.</p>
            <p>The translation by <m>\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}</m> takes the image back.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-road-arrows">
        <caption>Three arrows painted along a lane, seen from above.</caption>
        <image width="30%">
            <shortdescription>A lane drawn from above with three identical painted arrows; the tips are at (3, 4), (3, 19) and (3, 34), each 15 m beyond the last.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.3cm, y=0.1cm]
\fill[gray!15] (0,-2) rectangle (6,38);
\draw[thick] (0,-2) -- (0,38); \draw[thick] (6,-2) -- (6,38);
\foreach \t in {4,19,34} {
  \fill (2.4,\t-9) rectangle (3.6,\t-3);
  \fill (1.5,\t-3) -- (4.5,\t-3) -- (3,\t) -- cycle;
  \node[right] at (6.2,\t) {\scriptsize $(3, \t)$};
}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.5,26) -- (-1.5,36) node[midway, left] {\scriptsize N};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="example-arrows-along-a-lane">
        <title>Arrows Along a Lane</title>
        <statement>
            <p>A road crew paints arrows along a lane (<xref ref="fig-road-arrows"/>). On their plan, in metres, the tip of the first arrow is at <m>(3, 4)</m>, and each next arrow is the previous one translated by <m>\mathbf{T} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 15 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Find the tips of the second and third arrows, and the single translation that takes the first arrow to the third.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>Second tip: <m>(3, 4 + 15) = (3, 19)</m>. Third tip: <m>(3, 19 + 15) = (3, 34)</m>.</p>
            <p>The first arrow goes to the third by <m>\mathbf{T} + \mathbf{T} = 2\mathbf{T} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 30 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
            <p>Lane arrows tell drivers and riders which way each lane goes. Keeping to them prevents head-on crashes, so they are painted again and again along the road, each one a translation of the last.</p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

    <p>Type one number in each box of a column vector: the top box is the move across, the bottom box the move up. Use a minus sign for left or down, for example <c>-3</c>. Drag each point on the grid; it jumps to the nearest grid point. Every point must be moved before you check.</p>

