    <paragraphs xml:id="paragraphs-vectors-in-everyday-life">
        <title>Vectors Around Us</title>
        <p>
            We began the chapter by asking where vectors are used in everyday life. Here are some answers.
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li><p>Road arrows and direction signs give every road user a direction as well as a distance. The arrows along a lane and the stripes of a zebra crossing are translations of one shape.</p></li>
                <li><p>Engineers add the forces on a handcart, a bridge or a building to find the resultant force each part must carry.</p></li>
                <li><p>A weather forecast gives the wind's speed and direction: a velocity. Rain clouds are moved by the wind.</p></li>
                <li><p>A phone's map gives directions such as <q>in <m>200\,\text{m}</m> turn right</q>, and works out straight-line distances from coordinates.</p></li>
                <li><p>Computer games move a character on the screen by adding vectors many times a second.</p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            Where else in your community could you use a vector to describe a move, a push or a direction?
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Curriculum Links</title>
        <dl>
            <li><title>Key Inquiry Question</title><p>Where do we use vectors in everyday life?</p></li>
            <li><title>Core Competencies</title><p>Learning to learn, as learners find the rules for adding, multiplying and translating in the activities; self-efficacy, through the graded exercises and the Challenge items.</p></li>
            <li><title>Values</title><p>Unity, in group walks and measuring; respect, in pair work on notation and in listening to each group's report.</p></li>
            <li><title>Pertinent and Contemporary Issues</title><p>Road safety: road signs (<xref ref="sec-vector-and-scalar-quantities"/>), keeping to marked roads (<xref ref="example-boda-boda-route"/>) and lane arrows (<xref ref="example-arrows-along-a-lane"/>).</p></li>
            <li><title>Learning Outcome</title><p>This lesson and the closing paragraphs carry the outcome on appreciating the use of vectors in real-life situations.</p></li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-translation-vector">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-translate-two-points">
            <statement><p>Find the image of <m>A(2, 5)</m> under the translation by <m>\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}</m>, and of <m>B(-1, 0)</m> under the translation by <m>\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>A'(5, 1)</m>; <m>B'(-3, 6)</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-translate-a-triangle">
            <statement><p>Translate the triangle <m>P(1, 2)</m>, <m>Q(4, 2)</m>, <m>R(1, 6)</m> by <m>\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Check that the side lengths are unchanged.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>P'(-4, -1)</m>, <m>Q'(-1, -1)</m>, <m>R'(-4, 3)</m>; the sides are <m>3</m>, <m>4</m> and <m>5</m> in both triangles.</p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-find-the-translation">
            <statement><p>A translation maps <m>M(3, -2)</m> to <m>M'(-1, 4)</m>. Find the translation vector.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>\begin{pmatrix} -4 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-object-from-image">
            <statement><p>Under the translation by <m>\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}</m>, the image of <m>N</m> is <m>N'(0, 3)</m>. Find <m>N</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>N(-4, 4)</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-lane-arrows">
            <statement><p>On a road plan in metres, the tip of a lane arrow is at <m>(1, 3)</m>. Each next arrow is the previous one translated by <m>\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 12 \end{pmatrix}</m>. Where is the tip of the fourth arrow?</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>(1, 39)</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-two-translations">
            <title>Challenge: Two Translations</title>
            <statement><p>Translate <m>A(2, 1)</m> by <m>\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}</m> to <m>A'</m>, then translate <m>A'</m> by <m>\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}</m> to <m>A''</m>. Which single translation takes <m>A</m> straight to <m>A''</m>?</p></statement>
            <hint><p>Add the two vectors.</p></hint>
            <answer><p><m>A'(5, 5)</m>, <m>A''(0, 7)</m>; the translation by <m>\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m>.</p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>A'(2 + 3, 1 + 4) = (5, 5)</m> and <m>A''(5 - 5, 5 + 2) = (0, 7)</m>. The single translation is the sum <m>\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix}</m>; check: <m>(2 - 2, 1 + 6) = (0, 7)</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-translation-vector">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
